the coat here's another amazing rescue  the coast guard raced bad weather to  rescue all 11 injured survivors from a  plane crash crash in a remote and rugged  part of Alaska the sightseeing plane had  taken off from Prince of Wales Island  near the Gulf of Alaska it was headed to  Ketchikan but went down about 40 miles  southwest of its destination Kris Van  Cleave shows us the dramatic survival  story  Kris good morning good morning it really  was a race to get to these people the  weather was deteriorating and visibility  had dropped to only about a quarter of a  mile when the rescue chopper spotted the  downed plane moments after the Tuesday  morning crash the pilot used his  cellphone to call 911 that he and his 10  passengers all survived but many were  hurt and needed help the single-engine  float equipped two Haviland Otter  crashed about 2,000 feet up a mountain  in rocky terrain it took hours for the  two Coast Guard rescue helicopters to  find them all 11 people had to be  hoisted off the mountain into a chopper  and rushed to a staging area away from  the worsening weather the injuries are  said to be minor something a spokesman  for the Alaska state trooper says  frankly it's tremendous that no one was  killed  Taquan Air has video of the plane on its  Instagram the company owns about a dozen  aircraft it uses largely for sightseeing  tours out of Ketchikan it purchased  another tour operator after a deadly  2015 crash in the same type of plane the  company says it is cooperating with the  NTSB investigation but intends to resume  flying today  Alex dramatic story thank you Chris
 

